1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,400
Somebody says the weather is depressing. Somebody
else says the quarterly results are depressing.

2
00:00:06,750 --> 00:00:14,100
A third person, who has not left their flat in nine
days and cannot remember why they used to like music,

3
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:16,200
says they are a bit depressed.

4
00:00:16,550 --> 00:00:22,175
All three used the same word. Only
one of them was describing an illness.

5
00:00:22,525 --> 00:00:27,736
Around two hundred and eighty million
people are living with that illness.

6
00:00:27,736 --> 00:00:35,200
The World Health Organization ranks depressive
disorders among the leading causes of disability
worldwide.

7
00:00:35,550 --> 00:00:40,450
Not among mental illnesses.
Among all causes, of any kind.

8
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:45,950
So it is worth being precise about
what the word is supposed to mean.

9
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:50,920
The first thing to clear away is
that depression is not sadness.

10
00:00:50,920 --> 00:00:56,550
Treating it as sadness with the volume
turned up gets almost everything wrong.

11
00:00:56,900 --> 00:00:58,508
Sadness has an object.

12
00:00:58,508 --> 00:01:05,159
You are sad about something, and when the
something resolves, the sadness resolves with it.

13
00:01:05,159 --> 00:01:07,425
Sadness is also, oddly, useful.

14
00:01:07,775 --> 00:01:10,910
It slows you down. It makes you seek company.

15
00:01:10,910 --> 00:01:15,300
It signals to other people that
they should be gentle with you.

16
00:01:15,650 --> 00:01:23,047
Depression frequently has no object at all. And the
symptom clinicians look for first is not sadness.

17
00:01:23,047 --> 00:01:27,075
It is anhedonia. The loss of
the capacity for pleasure.

18
00:01:27,425 --> 00:01:34,324
Anhedonia is a much stranger thing than sadness,
and much harder to describe from the outside.

19
00:01:34,324 --> 00:01:37,700
Food tastes like it is being explained to you.

20
00:01:38,050 --> 00:01:45,425
Music becomes a sequence of correct notes. You can
remember loving something without being able to feel
it.

21
00:01:45,775 --> 00:01:53,150
Some people with severe depression report that they
cannot feel sad either. The condition took that as
well.

22
00:01:53,500 --> 00:02:00,975
None of this is a modern discovery. Physicians
described it as melancholia for two thousand years.

23
00:02:01,325 --> 00:02:07,288
Albrecht Durer engraved it in fifteen
fourteen: a winged figure sitting motionless,

24
00:02:07,288 --> 00:02:13,250
surrounded by every instrument of measurement
and construction, using none of them.

25
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:18,696
It is one of the most accurate portraits
of the condition anybody has made,

26
00:02:18,696 --> 00:02:22,025
and it predates the word depression by centuries.

27
00:02:22,375 --> 00:02:24,857
So if it isn't sadness, what is it?

28
00:02:24,857 --> 00:02:30,459
Here is where almost everyone, quite
reasonably, reaches for the same sentence.

29
00:02:30,459 --> 00:02:33,225
It's a chemical imbalance in the brain.

30
00:02:33,575 --> 00:02:35,600
Low serotonin.

31
00:02:35,950 --> 00:02:41,282
That sentence has a history, and the
history is not the one you would expect.

32
00:02:41,282 --> 00:02:43,775
It did not come out of a laboratory.

33
00:02:44,125 --> 00:02:47,825
It came, in large part, out of advertising.

34
00:02:48,175 --> 00:02:53,894
Through the late eighties and the nineties,
the drugs we now call SSRIs arrived,

35
00:02:53,894 --> 00:02:57,825
and with them a campaign to
explain them to the public.

36
00:02:58,175 --> 00:03:02,650
The explanation had to fit
inside a television commercial.

37
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,976
A chemical imbalance in the brain
fits inside a television commercial.

38
00:03:07,976 --> 00:03:13,450
It is short, it is mechanical, and it
puts the illness somewhere respectable.

39
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:16,850
In the body, rather than in the character.

40
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:22,630
It was a simplification from the beginning,
and researchers said so at the time.

41
00:03:22,630 --> 00:03:28,400
But it was an extremely good simplification,
in the commercial sense, and it escaped.

42
00:03:28,750 --> 00:03:36,775
In twenty twenty-two, a team led by Joanna Moncrieff
published an umbrella review in Molecular Psychiatry.

43
00:03:37,125 --> 00:03:38,507
A study of studies,

44
00:03:38,507 --> 00:03:45,051
gathering the evidence for the serotonin
hypothesis across six separate lines of research.

45
00:03:45,051 --> 00:03:50,650
They found no consistent evidence that
depression is caused by low serotonin.

46
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:57,929
The press treated this as a demolition. Inside
the field, the reaction was closer to a shrug,

47
00:03:57,929 --> 00:04:02,400
and the review itself drew
serious methodological criticism.

48
00:04:02,750 --> 00:04:07,067
The simple version had been
regarded as inadequate for years.

49
00:04:07,067 --> 00:04:11,525
What the review demolished was
the commercial, not the science.

50
00:04:11,875 --> 00:04:18,075
And here is the part that got mangled almost
everywhere, so it is worth saying slowly.

51
00:04:18,425 --> 00:04:24,358
Depression is not simply low serotonin
does not mean antidepressants do not work.

52
00:04:24,358 --> 00:04:26,775
Those are two separate questions.

53
00:04:27,125 --> 00:04:32,430
Aspirin works on headaches. Headaches
are not caused by a lack of aspirin.

54
00:04:32,430 --> 00:04:36,875
A drug does not have to reverse
a deficiency in order to help.

55
00:04:37,225 --> 00:04:44,525
If you are taking medication and it is helping you,
a paper about serotonin is not a reason to stop.

56
00:04:44,875 --> 00:04:47,400
So what is actually happening?

57
00:04:47,750 --> 00:04:52,832
The honest answer is that depression
is not one condition with one cause,

58
00:04:52,832 --> 00:04:58,750
which is precisely why the single-sentence
explanation was so appealing and so wrong.

59
00:04:59,100 --> 00:05:00,596
But there are patterns,

60
00:05:00,596 --> 00:05:06,775
and they are better described as a system stuck
in the wrong mode than as a missing ingredient.

61
00:05:07,125 --> 00:05:10,123
The clearest of them is the stress system.

62
00:05:10,123 --> 00:05:16,975
The body runs a hormonal cascade through the
hypothalamus, the pituitary and the adrenal glands.

63
00:05:17,325 --> 00:05:22,700
It produces cortisol, and then, when the
threat has passed, it shuts itself off.

64
00:05:23,050 --> 00:05:28,427
In a substantial share of depressed
patients, the shutting off is impaired.

65
00:05:28,427 --> 00:05:31,725
The alarm keeps sounding, quietly, for months.

66
00:05:32,075 --> 00:05:37,125
Cortisol is useful for an afternoon
and corrosive over a year.

67
00:05:37,475 --> 00:05:42,533
Chronic elevation is associated with
reduced volume in the hippocampus,

68
00:05:42,533 --> 00:05:49,800
the structure that handles memory and helps regulate
the very stress response that is wearing it down.

69
00:05:50,150 --> 00:05:52,650
A loop that tightens on itself.

70
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:56,187
The second pattern is in the reward circuitry.

71
00:05:56,187 --> 00:06:02,561
Dopamine-carrying pathways running from the
ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens,

72
00:06:02,561 --> 00:06:06,025
and their conversation with the prefrontal cortex.

73
00:06:06,375 --> 00:06:11,600
This is the machinery that converts
I want that into I am getting up.

74
00:06:11,950 --> 00:06:17,883
Under sustained stress, that conversion
degrades. Notice carefully what this means.

75
00:06:17,883 --> 00:06:22,600
It is not that the person wants
to get up and lacks the willpower.

76
00:06:22,950 --> 00:06:28,743
It is that the step where wanting turns
into doing has stopped working reliably.

77
00:06:28,743 --> 00:06:32,725
Anhedonia is that failure,
experienced from the inside.

78
00:06:33,075 --> 00:06:39,600
The third pattern is plasticity. The brain's
capacity to remodel its own connections.

79
00:06:39,950 --> 00:06:43,960
Depression is associated with
a measurable reduction in it,

80
00:06:43,960 --> 00:06:50,350
and several treatments that work appear to work
by restoring it, over weeks rather than hours.

81
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:53,071
That timescale is itself a clue.

82
00:06:53,071 --> 00:06:58,925
It is the timescale of something being
rebuilt, not of a level being topped up.

83
00:06:59,275 --> 00:07:07,350
The fourth is inflammation. In a subset of patients,
and it is a subset, inflammatory markers are elevated.

84
00:07:07,700 --> 00:07:13,662
Inducing inflammation experimentally in
healthy volunteers produces the flat,

85
00:07:13,662 --> 00:07:21,250
unmotivated state of depression, along with changes
in exactly the reward circuits just described.

86
00:07:21,600 --> 00:07:27,900
Which suggests there are biologically distinct
conditions currently wearing the same name.

87
00:07:28,250 --> 00:07:34,475
Now the consequences. This is where the
casual use of the word does the most damage.

88
00:07:34,825 --> 00:07:42,050
Depression is not only felt. It measurably degrades
the things people are held responsible for.

89
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:48,614
Concentration, working memory, decision
speed and mental flexibility all decline.

90
00:07:48,614 --> 00:07:54,675
From the outside, this looks exactly like
not trying. Someone misses deadlines.

91
00:07:55,025 --> 00:08:01,375
Forgets what was agreed. Takes four days to answer
an email that would have taken four minutes.

92
00:08:01,725 --> 00:08:09,125
The available explanations are ill and lazy, and
the second one requires no imagination at all.

93
00:08:09,475 --> 00:08:11,975
It does not stay in the head either.

94
00:08:12,325 --> 00:08:20,197
Depression is associated with higher cardiovascular
risk and with worse outcomes for people already
managing

95
00:08:20,197 --> 00:08:25,300
a physical illness, in ways that
lifestyle alone does not account for.

96
00:08:25,650 --> 00:08:28,802
And it carries a mortality risk of its own.

97
00:08:28,802 --> 00:08:34,300
Untreated depression is among the
strongest predictors of suicide there is.

98
00:08:34,650 --> 00:08:42,656
The World Health Organization puts the combined
economic cost of depression and anxiety at up to a
trillion

99
00:08:42,656 --> 00:08:45,350
dollars a year in lost productivity.

100
00:08:45,700 --> 00:08:50,607
That figure is usually quoted to
make the case for funding treatment.

101
00:08:50,607 --> 00:08:58,075
It also quietly describes several hundred million
people unable to do work they are otherwise capable
of.

102
00:08:58,425 --> 00:09:03,525
Which brings us to the part that is
about us, rather than about neurons.

103
00:09:03,875 --> 00:09:11,350
We have assembled a set of expectations around this
illness that we would find absurd applied to any
other.

104
00:09:11,700 --> 00:09:19,229
We ask people whose reward circuitry has stopped
converting motivation into action to be more
motivated.

105
00:09:19,229 --> 00:09:23,500
We recommend exercise to someone
who cannot start a shower.

106
00:09:23,850 --> 00:09:29,175
We tell them to reach out, and the reaching
out costs more than they currently have.

107
00:09:29,525 --> 00:09:36,675
And the strange part is that the advice is not
even wrong. Exercise has real evidence behind it.

108
00:09:37,025 --> 00:09:42,636
Behavioural activation, deliberately doing
things before you feel like doing them,

109
00:09:42,636 --> 00:09:46,400
is one of the better-supported
treatments in the field.

110
00:09:46,750 --> 00:09:50,019
The problem was never the content of the advice.

111
00:09:50,019 --> 00:09:56,080
It is handing somebody the operating instructions
for a machine that is currently broken,

112
00:09:56,080 --> 00:09:58,600
and recording that as help delivered.

113
00:09:58,950 --> 00:10:01,166
Then there is the word itself.

114
00:10:01,166 --> 00:10:07,666
A word that has to cover both a rainy Tuesday
and a disabling illness will, in practice,

115
00:10:07,666 --> 00:10:10,325
get calibrated to the rainy Tuesday.

116
00:10:10,675 --> 00:10:15,125
That is simply how language
works. The common meaning wins.

117
00:10:15,475 --> 00:10:19,776
Which means that every time somebody
says the real thing out loud,

118
00:10:19,776 --> 00:10:25,250
they are arguing with the definition before
they even reach the end of the sentence.

119
00:10:25,600 --> 00:10:30,150
Depression is not sadness, and
it is not a missing chemical.

120
00:10:30,500 --> 00:10:36,890
It is a set of systems, stress regulation,
motivation, plasticity, sometimes immunity,

121
00:10:36,890 --> 00:10:41,125
that have settled into a state
they cannot leave unaided.

122
00:10:41,475 --> 00:10:43,633
It is common. It is treatable.

123
00:10:43,633 --> 00:10:49,675
And it is far more mechanical, and far less
voluntary, than the word makes it sound.

124
00:10:50,025 --> 00:10:55,075
The word is the problem. The people
using it about the weather are not.

125
00:10:55,425 --> 00:10:58,828
But the next time somebody
tells you they are depressed,

126
00:10:58,828 --> 00:11:03,325
it costs about four seconds to find
out which of the two things they mean.
